# Chroma content pack Stage and Screen: THRESHOLD SEASONS (moments 74 to 89 of MOMENTS-BRIEF.md, Part 6). Four short
# seasons of four linked moments each, for the first months after a person takes drama school student, professional
# actor, lead actor or actress, or director. The pack's crossings ('drama school auditions', 'the showcase for agents',
# 'an open audition in the city', 'the understudy goes on', 'the part of a lifetime is cast', 'a screen test', 'the
# leading player leaves the company', 'opening night at the fringe', 'the lead voice in a series', 'the director calls
# again', 'two actors, one part', 'a director is needed for the autumn play') are the rites that open them; any other way
# into the title opens the season too. Emren's ask is the heart of it ("Lead actor/actresss availability must be inside
# with all new content"): the lead's season shows what the summit is like, the first weeks of top billing, the company
# that now looks to the lead, whom the lead plays it for, and the morning after the last night. Every season asks whom
# the person now answers to: the year group, the people at home, a partner who sees them less, the company.
# Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py, plus the season fields the engine accepted (chroma-packs/for-the-engine.md,
# "Engine answer"): threshold: title:<name>, step: 1 | 2 | 3 (the engine brings them about 1, 10 and 22 weeks after the
# title is gained, inside a season of about 30 weeks), transform: yes (the season's transforming chance, which the engine
# plays in a week of its own, about 16 weeks in). Every moment: tier inner, alpha even, stakes .8, holds: the title, the
# five one-color options and one full cross cycle; each season uses the cycles s1 to s4 once, so each cycle comes four
# times. Worlds (Emren, 21:37): every moment has earth:, tribal: and magic: lines in the forms of worlds.py, an untagged
# tribal and magic scene, and option wording where the act reads differently there; no act here is a magic act.
# Rules: chroma-library/library-spec.md. Pathways and packs thread, 2026-10-06.
# Two departures from the brief, on purpose: moment 81 is 'half a year on, still an actor' (the brief's 'a year on'),
# and the step 3 times lines say about five months in, since the engine brings step 3 about 22 weeks after the title.
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (checklist P14, value tags): 6 options in this file retagged so the v: values read one of the
# option's own colors (check.py section 4): White's by-the-book singles lead with conformity (not conformity with
# achievement, which reads Blue), Red's singles on impulse lead with stimulation or hedonism (not self-direction,
# which reads Black), Green's singles of home and kin with tradition and benevolence or universalism (not security,
# which reads White), and Red's kindness on the spot with benevolence and stimulation.

# ================================================================ drama school student (ages 16-40)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 74. step 1 (cycle s1)

== the first morning of the first term
tier: inner
threshold: title:drama school student
step: 1
stages: juvenile young_adult adult
age: 16-40
times: per new drama school student: once, in the first weeks after taking the title; about 3 lives in 1,000 (catalogue share)
holds: drama school student
stakes: .8
tone: joy
life: learning, friends
horizon: week
roles: mentor, colleague, parent
earth: the first morning at drama school: a converted warehouse, a timetable from nine to nine, and thirty strangers in black
tribal: the first morning at an old teller's fire in another band, far from your own hearth, with the other apprentices watching
magic: the first morning in the guild school's hall: its masters, its rules, and a bench among strangers in the guild's grey
requires: in the threshold season of drama school student, within the first six months after taking the title (opened by 'drama school auditions' or any other way in): step 1, the first weeks of the course
likelier: a first place won at the auditions; a student who moved to a new city for the course; a full-time course of three years
rarer: a part-time or foundation course; a student who has already trained somewhere else
scene: The first morning of drama school starts in a circle on the floor of a converted warehouse: thirty strangers in black, each saying a name and one thing nobody knows about them. The timetable runs from nine in the morning to nine at night, and {mentor}, the head of acting, says the first year is for taking everything apart.
scene W: {N} has bought every book on the reading list, labelled the rehearsal clothes and arrived twenty minutes early.
scene U: {N} wants to know what each class is for: the voice, the movement, the text, and how they fit together into an actor.
scene B: {N} sizes up the year group: who already has an agent's eye on them, who came from the famous youth company, who is the one to beat.
scene R: {N} is giddy. Three years of nothing but acting, and the circle on the floor feels like the start of everything.
scene G: {N} thinks of the kitchen at home, of {parent} driving {N} to the auditions, and of a town that already seems very far away.
scene tribal: At dawn {N} sits for the first time at an old teller's fire in another band, far from home, and the other apprentices watch to see what the newcomer can do.
scene magic: In the guild school's great hall the masters stand in their robes, a clerk reads out the rules, and {N} takes a bench among strangers in the guild's grey.
- W | learn everyone's name by lunchtime, and keep every rule of the studio | v: conformity, benevolence | tribal: learn every apprentice's name by midday, and keep every custom of the old teller's fire | magic: learn every scholar's name by the noon bell, and keep every rule of the hall | chance: 80
- U | ask the head of acting what each class of the first year is for | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: ask the old teller what each part of the first winter is meant to teach | magic: ask the master of the school what each class of the first year is for | chance: 88
- B | find out which teachers the agents listen to, and get noticed by them | v: power, achievement | tribal: find out whose word the elders of other bands trust, and get noticed by the old teller | magic: find out which masters the players' brokers listen to, and get noticed by them | chance: 80
- R | throw yourself into the first improvisation game, and make the whole room laugh | v: stimulation, hedonism | tribal: leap into the first game at the fire, and make the whole circle laugh | chance: 80
- G | ring home at the end of the day, and tell them every detail | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: send word home with a trader going upriver that the first day went well | magic: send a letter home by the evening coach, with every detail of the day | chance: 92
- W>U | come in early every morning to warm up properly, as the movement teacher asked | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: rise before the others every morning to warm the voice, as the old teller asked | magic: come to the hall before the first bell every morning to warm up, as the master asked | chance: 82
- U>B | read the year above's notes on every teacher, and plan how to stand out | v: achievement, power | tribal: ask the older apprentices about the old teller's moods, and plan how to stand out | magic: buy the year above's notes on every master, and plan how to stand out | chance: 65
- B>R | throw a party for the whole year group in your new flat on the first Friday | v: hedonism, power | tribal: gather the apprentices for a night of meat and stories at the first full moon | magic: throw a supper for the whole year in your lodgings on the first rest day | chance: 90
- R>G | find the other student who looks lost in the canteen, and sit with them | door | v: benevolence | mark: made a friend | tribal: find the other apprentice who is far from home, and share your fire with them | magic: find the other scholar who looks lost at the long table, and sit with them | chance: 92
- G>W | write to the youth theatre leader who started it all, and promise to make them proud | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: send word to the old one of your own band who taught you, and promise to honour them | magic: write to the children's master who started it all, and promise to make them proud | chance: 88
works: By the end of the first week {N} knows the building, the timetable and most of the names, and the warehouse begins to feel like a place to work.
works: {mentor} stops {N} in the corridor on Friday to say it was a good first week.
fails: The first week is a blur of names and warm-ups, and {N} goes home each night too tired to remember any of it.
fails: Something {N} said in the first circle is the joke of the year group by Friday, and not a kind one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 75. step 2 (cycle s2)

== the movement class where you cannot hide
tier: inner
threshold: title:drama school student
step: 2
stages: juvenile young_adult adult
age: 16-40
times: per new drama school student: once, in the first months of the threshold season, about ten weeks in; about 3 lives in 1,000 (catalogue share)
holds: drama school student
stakes: .8
tone: trouble
life: learning, body
horizon: months
roles: mentor, colleague
earth: ten weeks in, a bare studio, bare feet and no script, and the movement teacher asks each student to stand in the middle and simply be looked at
tribal: two moons in, the old teller makes you dance the bear alone in the firelight, with no words to hide behind, while the other apprentices watch
magic: ten weeks in, the guild's master of movement makes you walk the length of the hall in silence before the whole year, with no mask and no lines
requires: in the threshold season of drama school student, within the first six months after taking the title: step 2, some weeks into the course, when the classes start to ask for more than talent
likelier: a course with daily movement and voice classes; a student who has always hidden behind a character; a student from a family or a school where nobody acted
rarer: a student who trained as a dancer or an athlete; a part-time course with few movement classes
scene: Ten weeks in, movement is the class {N} dreads. Bare feet, no script, and today {mentor} asks each student to stand alone in the middle of the studio for two minutes and do nothing while the year group watches. {N} is next.
scene W: {N} decides that it is an exercise like any other, set for a reason, and that the rules of the room protect everyone in it.
scene U: {N} has read about this exercise and knows why it works. Knowing does not stop {Ns} legs from shaking.
scene B: {N} watches the others go first and notes what the teacher praises: stillness, breath, eyes up.
scene R: Being looked at with nothing to do makes {N} want to laugh, run and cry, all at once.
scene G: {N} thinks of the body {N} has always known: the walk from home, the way {Ns} family stands. Here it is being taken apart.
scene tribal: The old teller beats the drum slowly and points at {N}: dance the bear, alone, in the firelight, with no words, while the other apprentices watch.
scene magic: The master of movement strikes his staff on the floor, and {N} must walk the length of the hall in silence, without a mask, before the whole year.
- W | do exactly what is asked, and stand there for the full two minutes | v: conformity | tribal: do what the old teller asks, and dance until the drum stops | magic: do what the master asks, and walk the whole hall at his pace | chance: 85
- U | ask the teacher afterwards what the exercise is for, and keep a notebook on it | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | tribal: ask the old teller afterwards what the bear dance is for, and remember every word | chance: 90
- B | copy exactly what the teacher praised in the others, and give them that | v: achievement, power | tribal: copy what the old teller praised in the others, and give him that | magic: copy what the master praised in the others, and give him that | chance: 70
- R | let whatever comes come, tears or laughter, in front of everyone | v: stimulation, hedonism | tribal: let whatever comes come in the dance, tears or laughter, in front of everyone | magic: let whatever comes come on the walk, tears or laughter, in front of everyone | chance: 80
- G | breathe slowly and think of a place at home until the fear passes | v: tradition, security | self_control: + | tribal: breathe slowly and think of your own band's river until the fear passes | magic: breathe slowly and think of the lane where you grew up until the fear passes | chance: 70
- W>B | volunteer to go first, as a good student should, and earn the teacher's eye | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: step into the firelight first, as a good apprentice should, and earn the old teller's eye | magic: volunteer to walk first, as a good scholar should, and earn the master's eye | chance: 60
- U>R | make a private game of it: how little can you do and still hold the room | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: make a private game of it: how little can the bear do and still hold the band's eyes | chance: 78
- B>G | fake a twisted ankle to sit it out, and take the train home for the weekend | v: security, hedonism | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: lying to a teacher to miss a class; backfire: the movement teacher sees through it, and the year group hears | self_control: - | tribal: say your foot is hurt to sit out the dance, and walk home to your own band for a few days | magic: plead a twisted ankle to sit out the walk, and take the coach home for the rest day | chance: 85
- R>W | step up beside a classmate who freezes, so they are not alone | v: benevolence, universalism | mark: helped someone in need | tribal: step into the firelight beside an apprentice who froze, and dance with them | magic: walk beside a scholar who froze, so they are not alone | chance: 65
- G>U | find the walk of someone from home in your body, and use it | v: tradition, achievement | tribal: dance the bear the way the old hunters of your band move, and use it | magic: walk the way your grandmother walked down the lane, and use it | chance: 72
works: {N} stands in the middle and is looked at for two minutes, and the floor does not open.
works: {mentor} nods once at {N}, which in that studio counts as high praise.
fails: {N} freezes, and the two minutes feel like a week.
fails: {N} gets through the class and cries on the bus home, and the next movement class comes round all the same.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 76. step 2, transform (cycle s3)

== who you are when you are not acting
tier: inner
threshold: title:drama school student
step: 2
transform: yes
stages: juvenile young_adult adult
age: 16-40
times: per new drama school student: once in the threshold season, about four months in, for most who are still open to change; about 3 lives in 1,000 (catalogue share)
holds: drama school student
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: learning, meaning, friends
horizon: years
roles: mentor, friend, parent
earth: four months in, a teacher asks the year who each of them is when they are not acting, and the school has started to swallow everything else
tribal: by midwinter, the old teller asks each apprentice what they will be when the winter is over, and your own band feels very far away
magic: by midwinter, the masters ask each scholar who they will be when the masks come off, and the life before the guild feels very far away
requires: in the threshold season of drama school student, within the first six months after taking the title: step 2, the season's transforming chance, when the course has begun to take over the rest of life
likelier: a student who moved away from home for the course; old friends and a partner from before; a teacher who asks the hard question out loud
rarer: a student who lives at home through the course; an older student with a life already settled
scene: Four months in, {mentor} stops a class and asks the year a question: who is each of them when they are not acting? Nobody answers well. On the bus home {N} sees that the school has taken over everything: the friends from before have stopped calling, {parent} asks on the phone what all this is for, and the year group has become the only people {N} sees.
scene W: {N} thinks of the year group as a company already: thirty people who will carry each other through three years, or not at all.
scene U: {N} thinks the answer is in the work: the voice, the body, the text. An actor is a craftsman, and the craft is enough.
scene B: {N} counts: three years, then the showcase, then a hundred agents in the seats. Everything here is a step toward that night.
scene R: {N} came here because acting is the only time {N} feels fully alive, and that is answer enough.
scene G: {N} thinks of home: the youth theatre, the town, the people who will come to see whatever {N} becomes. Whoever {N} is, {N} is from there.
scene tribal: By midwinter the old teller asks each apprentice what they will be when the winter is over. {N} looks at this band's fire and thinks of {Ns} own, far up the river.
scene magic: At the midwinter masque the masters ask each scholar who they will be when the masks come off, and {N} finds that the life before the guild has grown very faint.
- W | give yourself to the year group, and be the one the company can always count on | identity | v: benevolence, conformity | tribal: give yourself to the apprentices, and be the one the others can always count on | magic: give yourself to your year, and be the scholar the others can always count on | chance: 80
- U | make the craft your life: voice, body and text, every day, for good | identity | habit | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: make the telling your life: the voices, the dances and the old words, every day, for good | chance: 65
- B | treat the three years as a plan for the showcase, and spend every choice on it | identity | v: achievement, power | self_control: + | tribal: treat the three winters as a road to the summer gathering, where every band's elders will see you | magic: treat the three years as a road to the final masque, where the brokers sit in the gallery | chance: 85
- R | swear to act or do nothing, and burn every other bridge | identity | binds | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | tribal: swear to be a teller or nothing, and turn your back on the hunt for good | magic: swear to be a player or nothing, and turn your back on your old trade for good | chance: 72
- G | stay who you were at home, and bring that person into every part | identity | v: tradition, self-direction | tribal: stay a child of your own band, its speech and its ways, and bring them into every telling | magic: stay who you were in your own lane, speech and all, and bring it into every part | chance: 70
- W>R | promise the year group a show at the fringe together when the course ends | identity | binds | v: benevolence, stimulation | tribal: swear with the other apprentices to play together at the outer fires one summer | magic: swear with your year to take a wagon to the fair together when the course is done | chance: 45
- U>G | join the school's three-year project collecting the old plays of your region | identity | binds | v: tradition, achievement | tribal: learn every telling of your own band by heart, and promise to carry them on | magic: join the school's long work of collecting the old pageants of your home country | chance: 85
- B>W | stand for year rep, and run the year group's dealings with the school | identity | binds | v: power, conformity | tribal: become the one who speaks for the apprentices to the old teller | magic: stand for the year's speaker, and run its dealings with the masters | chance: 45
- R>U | throw out everything from before, and let the teachers start again from nothing | identity | v: stimulation, achievement | tribal: forget every way of playing from home, and let the old teller start again from nothing | magic: throw out every habit from before, and let the masters start again from nothing | chance: 70
- G>B | keep the weekend job back home, so you owe nobody and can always go back | identity | binds | v: security, power | tribal: keep your place in your own band's hunts, so you owe nobody and can always go back | magic: keep your old trade at home on rest days, so you owe nobody and can always go back | chance: 72
works: Before the term ends {N} has an answer to the question, and the work starts to feel like {Ns} own.
works: {friend} from home comes to the end-of-term sharing and says {N} is more like {N} than ever.
fails: Every answer {N} tries sounds false by the next morning, and the question follows {N} into every class.
fails: The old friends drift away, the school swallows the rest, and {N} is no longer sure whose life this is.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 77. step 3 (cycle s4)

== the first-year assessment
tier: inner
threshold: title:drama school student
step: 3
stages: juvenile young_adult adult
age: 16-40
times: per new drama school student: once, about five months in, at the first-year assessment near the end of the threshold season; about 3 lives in 1,000 (catalogue share); a few schools ask some first-year students to leave (estimate)
holds: drama school student
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: learning, family
horizon: months
roles: mentor, parent, colleague
earth: the first-year assessment: a speech, a song and a movement piece before the staff panel, and a one-to-one that decides who goes on
tribal: near the end of the first winter, the old teller hears each apprentice tell the great story alone, and says who may stay for a second winter
magic: the first-year trial: each scholar plays before the guild's masters, and a clerk writes who stays on the roll
requires: in the threshold season of drama school student, within the first six months after taking the title: step 3, near the end of the six months, at the first-year assessment
likelier: a school that assesses its first-years and keeps the right to ask some to leave; a family that lent the fees; a student who had a warning in the first term
rarer: a school that never asks a student to leave; a student already picked out as one of the year's best
scene: At the first-year assessment every student performs a speech, a song and a movement piece for the staff panel, then sits in a small room with {mentor} for ten minutes that decide the second year. Two of last year's group were not asked back. {parent}, who lent the money for the fees, has rung twice to ask when {N} will know.
scene W: {N} has kept every note from every class in a folder, and has done everything the teachers asked, in order.
scene U: {N} knows exactly which notes keep coming back (the breath, the jaw, the hurry) and has worked on each one.
scene B: {N} has noticed which students the head of acting talks about in the canteen, and {N} is not yet one of them.
scene R: {N} is sick of being careful. Five months of exercises, and {N} wants to just act.
scene G: {N} thinks of the family who lent the money, and of the people at home who keep asking how it is going.
scene tribal: Near the end of the first winter the old teller sits by the fire and hears each apprentice tell the great story alone. Two of last winter's apprentices were sent home after it.
scene magic: The masters sit in a row in the great hall, and a clerk waits with the roll to write down who stays.
- W | rehearse the set pieces exactly as taught, every evening for a month | habit | v: conformity, tradition | self_control: + | grants: learning lines | tribal: rehearse the great story exactly as the old teller tells it, every evening for a moon | magic: rehearse the set pieces exactly as the masters taught, every evening for a month | chance: 83
- U | work on the one note that keeps coming back, and nothing else | v: achievement | mark: learned a skill | tribal: work on the one fault the old teller keeps naming, and nothing else | chance: 60
- B | ask the head of acting for a private word about what the panel wants | v: power, achievement | tribal: sit with the old teller before the hearing, and find out what he wants to see | magic: seek out the head master before the trial, and find out what the masters want to see | chance: 70
- R | throw away the careful version, and play the speech as you feel it that day | v: stimulation | tribal: put aside the careful telling, and tell the story as it comes to you by the fire | chance: 60
- G | ask the family to come to the end-of-term sharing, and play it for them | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: ask your own kin to come down the river for the hearing, and tell it for them | magic: ask your family to come to the end-of-term playing, and play it for them | chance: 65
- W>G | spend the last week helping the classmates who are struggling, not only yourself | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | tribal: spend the last days helping the apprentices who are struggling, not only yourself | chance: 90
- U>W | write out every note from the panel this year, and answer each one in turn | v: conformity, achievement | self_control: + | tribal: go over every fault the old teller named all winter, and mend each in turn | magic: copy out every note the masters gave all year, and answer each one in turn | chance: 85
- B>U | borrow last year's best student's notes, and copy how they passed | door | v: achievement, power | tribal: ask last winter's best apprentice how they passed, and copy them | magic: buy last year's best scholar's notes, and copy how they passed | chance: 88
- R>B | skip the classes you find pointless, and pour the time into your acting piece | v: self-direction, achievement | drops_if_fails: drama school student | tribal: skip the dances you find pointless, and pour the days into your telling | magic: skip the lessons you find pointless, and pour the hours into your piece | chance: 85
- G>R | spend the weekend before at home, walking the old places, and come back fresh | v: hedonism, tradition | tribal: go back up the river to your own band before the hearing, and come back fresh | magic: spend the rest day at home walking the old lanes, and come back fresh | chance: 90
works: The panel keeps {N} on, with notes for the summer and a good word from {mentor}.
works: {parent} hears the news on the phone, goes quiet, then says they always knew.
fails: The verdict is hard: a warning at best, and {N} walks out of the small room shaking.
fails: The one-to-one is ten minutes of things {N} did not want to hear, and the rest of the year will have to prove them wrong.

# ================================================================ professional actor (ages 16-100)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 78. step 1 (cycle s2)

== the first paid read-through
tier: inner
threshold: title:professional actor
step: 1
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-100
times: per new professional actor: once, in the first weeks after taking the title; about 4 lives in 1,000 (catalogue share)
holds: professional actor
stakes: .8
tone: joy
life: work, friends
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague, partner
earth: the first paid job: a long table, a script with your name on the cast list, and a cup of tea you are too nervous to drink
tribal: the first winter as the band's teller-player, fed by every hearth, and the first night you sit among the tellers as one of them
magic: the first reading with a licensed company, and your name in small letters on the playbill
requires: in the threshold season of professional actor, within the first six months after taking the title (opened by 'the showcase for agents', 'an open audition in the city' or any other way in): step 1, the first paid job
likelier: a first paid part after drama school or an open call; a company of older actors; a director who reads the play round a table on the first day
rarer: a first job on a film set with no read-through; an actor who turned professional after years as an amateur in the same company
scene: The first day of the first paid job: a long table in a rehearsal room, a script with {Ns} name on the cast list, and a cup of tea {N} is too nervous to drink. Around the table sit actors {N} has watched for years. {boss}, the director, says a few words, and the read-through begins.
scene W: {N} has learned the first act already, marked every entrance and arrived forty minutes early.
scene U: {N} has read the play six times, has a question about every scene, and is not sure whether a new actor may ask them.
scene B: {N} notices who the director laughs with, which actor the producer came to see, and where the next job might come from.
scene R: {N} cannot stop grinning. Someone is paying {N} to do this.
scene G: {N} thinks of {partner} and the people at home who said it would never happen, and of the old company that taught {N} how.
scene tribal: For the first time {N} sits among the band's tellers as one of them, and every hearth has sent meat to the tellers' fire for the winter.
scene magic: The company gathers at a long table in the playhouse, the playbill on the wall carries {Ns} name in small letters, and the master of the play calls for the first reading.
- W | read exactly as you rehearsed at home, and mark every note in pencil | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: tell your part exactly as you learned it, and keep every word the shaper gives | magic: read exactly as you prepared it, and mark every note in the margin | chance: 90
- U | ask the director one good question about your character at the first break | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: ask the shaper one good question about your spirit at the first rest | magic: ask the master of the play one good question about your part at the first break | chance: 85
- B | sit next to the actor the producer came to see, and make them laugh | v: power, achievement | tribal: sit beside the teller the elders favour, and make them laugh | magic: sit beside the company's favourite player, and make them laugh | chance: 82
- R | read your big scene full out, as if it were the first night | v: stimulation, hedonism | tribal: tell your part full out, as if the whole band were watching | magic: read your big scene full out, as if the house were full | chance: 78
- G | bring a cake for the whole company, as your old company always did | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: bring a share of your own family's meat to the tellers' fire | magic: bring a basket of buns for the whole company, as your old pageant players always did | chance: 92
- W>B | learn the name of everyone in the company and crew before lunch | door | v: conformity, power | tribal: learn the name of every teller and every keeper of the fire before midday | magic: learn the name of every player and stagehand before the noon bell | chance: 75
- U>R | listen hard to the others, and enjoy every surprise the play holds | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: listen to the others' tellings more than your own, and enjoy every surprise in the story | chance: 90
- B>G | get cheap digs through the stage manager's contacts, and send the savings home | v: security, benevolence | tribal: get a place at a rich hearth through the keeper of the fire, and send your own meat home to your kin | magic: get cheap lodgings through the book-keeper's friends, and send the savings home | chance: 72
- R>W | admit at the first break that you have not finished learning the second act | v: benevolence, self-direction | mark: owned up | tribal: admit at the first rest that you do not yet know the second part of the telling | magic: admit at the first break that you have not yet learned the second act | chance: 80
- G>U | ask the oldest actor at the table how they have kept going for forty years | door | v: tradition, achievement | mark: made a friend | tribal: ask the oldest teller at the fire how they kept their voice for forty winters | magic: ask the company's oldest player how they have kept going for forty seasons | chance: 92
works: By the end of the read-through {N} has stopped shaking, and {boss} catches {Ns} eye at a good moment.
works: The company goes to the pub afterwards, and someone saves {N} a seat.
fails: {N} stumbles over the first line and never quite recovers, and the day goes by in a blur of nerves.
fails: Something {N} did at the table is remembered by the whole company, and not in a good way.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 79. step 2 (cycle s3)

== the second job does not come
tier: inner
threshold: title:professional actor
