- G>R | take the whole company to the park on a free afternoon, to play like children | v: hedonism, benevolence | body: light | tribal: take the players to the river on a free day, to swim and play like children | magic: take the company to the meadows on a rest day, to play like children | chance: 82
works: The company settles, the slack goes out of the show, and the dressing rooms are cheerful again by the end of the month.
works: {partner} says one Sunday that {N} seems to have found a way to lead and still come home.
fails: The quarrel spreads, the show goes flat, and the company blames the lead.
fails: {N} holds the company together and falls apart a little at home, too tired to talk.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 84. step 2, transform (cycle s1)

== who you play it for
tier: inner
threshold: title:lead actor or actress
step: 2
transform: yes
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-100
times: per new lead: once in the threshold season, about four months in, for most who are still open to change; about 1 life in 1,000 (catalogue share; estimate)
holds: lead actor or actress
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: work, meaning
horizon: years
roles: partner, colleague
earth: four months in, a long interview asks who you play the part for every night, and the true answer is not the one you gave
tribal: four moons in, the shaman sits by your fire and asks who you wear the great mask for, and the summer is half gone
magic: four months in, a broadsheet asks whom the leading player plays for, and the question will not leave you
requires: in the threshold season of lead actor or actress, within the first six months after taking the part: step 2, the season's transforming chance, once the run has shown what the lead costs and what it brings
likelier: a run that has gone well; an offer of the next part; an actor who came to the lead from far away
rarer: a run about to close early; an actor who has led many times before
scene: Four months in, a journalist doing a long interview asks a simple question: who does {N} play it for, every night? {N} gives a good answer for the article and lies awake afterwards, because it was not the true one. The run will end, and the next choices will show what the lead was for.
scene W: {N} thinks of the company: the understudy, the dressers, the stage manager calling the show. The lead serves them first.
scene U: {N} thinks of the part itself, and of the scene in the second act that {N} has still not got right after a hundred performances.
scene B: {N} thinks of what comes next: the film offer, the bigger agency, the next lead. This part is a door, and {N} means to go through it.
scene R: {N} thinks of the house: the moment the lights go down and eight hundred strangers hold their breath together. That is what it is for.
scene G: {N} thinks of the people from home who drove four hours to see it, and the old drama teacher in the third row, crying.
scene tribal: Four moons in, the shaman sits by {Ns} fire and asks who {N} wears the great mask for: the band, the ancestor, {Ns} own name, the other players or {Ns} own hearth.
scene magic: Four months in, a broadsheet asks whom the leading player plays for, and {N} finds that the question will not leave.
- W | play it for the company: lead by example, and see everyone through the run | identity | v: benevolence, conformity | tribal: wear it for the players: lead by example, and see every one of them through the summer | magic: play it for the company: lead by example, and see every player through the season | chance: 78
- U | play it for the part: a voice coach all run, and new work every night | identity | binds | habit | v: achievement, self-direction | self_control: + | tribal: wear it for the ancestor: sit with the old teller all summer, and find something new every night | magic: play it for the part: a master of voice for the season, and new work every night | chance: 72
- B | play it for the career: use the run to land the next and bigger part | identity | v: achievement, power | tribal: wear it for your name: use the summer to be asked for by every band | magic: play it for your name: use the season to win a place at the court playhouse | chance: 72
- R | play it for the house: give everything every night, as if it were the last | identity | v: stimulation, hedonism | self_control: + | tribal: wear it for the band: give everything every night, as if it were the last | chance: 78
- G | play it for the people at home, and go back to them between every job | identity | binds | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: wear it for your own hearth, and go back to them between every gathering | magic: play it for your own people, and go home to them between every season | chance: 72
- W>U | promise the director to keep the show exactly as it opened, every night of the run | identity | binds | v: conformity, achievement | mark: kept your word | tribal: swear to the shaper to keep the telling exactly as it was first told, every night | magic: swear to the master of the play to keep the show exactly as it opened, every night | chance: 62
- U>B | study the leads whose careers you want, and plan five years like a campaign | identity | v: achievement, power | tribal: study the great wearers of the mask, and plan your next five summers like a hunt | magic: study the great leading players of the realm, and plan five seasons like a campaign | chance: 65
- B>R | sign with the big agency, and say yes to fame: the interviews, the parties, the cameras | identity | binds | v: hedonism, power | tribal: let the go-between carry your name to every band, and take every feast and gift that comes | magic: sign with the grandest broker, and take every supper, every portrait and every crowd | chance: 70
- R>G | go out to the stage door every night of the run, for everyone who waited | identity | binds | v: benevolence, stimulation | tribal: go out among the families every night after the telling, for everyone who waited | magic: go out to the playhouse door every night, for everyone who waited | chance: 80
- G>W | keep the company's old customs: the warm-up circle, the half-hour toast, the last-night gift | identity | v: tradition, conformity | tribal: keep the tellers' old ways: the circle before the fire, the gift to the ancestor, the last song | magic: keep the company's old customs: the circle, the toast at the bell, the last-night gift | chance: 95
works: The answer holds, and the last months of the run feel like {Ns} own, whatever the notices say.
works: Long after, people who worked on the show can say what kind of lead {N} was, and they say it warmly.
fails: {N} tries to play it for everyone at once, and some nights it is played for nobody at all.
fails: The answer chosen costs more than {N} expected, and {partner} says so.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 85. step 3 (cycle s2)

== the last night of the run
tier: inner
threshold: title:lead actor or actress
step: 3
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-100
times: per new lead: once, about five months in, near the end of the run or of the threshold season; about 1 life in 1,000 (catalogue share; estimate)
holds: lead actor or actress
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: work, family, friends
horizon: months
roles: partner, colleague
earth: five months in, the last night of the run or the last day of filming: the flowers, the speech, the empty dressing room, and a diary with nothing in it
tribal: at the end of the summer gathering, the last telling: the great mask lifted from your face and hung back on its peg
magic: at the end of the season, the last night: the herald cries your name one more time, and the wagons are already packed
requires: in the threshold season of lead actor or actress, within the first six months after taking the part: step 3, near the end of the six months, when the run or the series ends
likelier: a fixed run of a few months; a series that finishes filming; a lead with nothing booked next
rarer: an open-ended run; a series renewed at once; a lead who goes straight into the next part
scene: Five months in, the last night. The house stands at the curtain call, the company gives {N} flowers and a card everyone has signed, and the set comes down overnight. The next morning there is no call, no car, no half-hour, and a diary with nothing in it. {partner} has planned a week away. {N} is not sure who {N} is without the part.
scene W: {N} wants to end it properly: a speech for the crew, a card for every dresser, the dressing room left clean.
scene U: {N} can feel what the part taught, and wants to write it down before it goes.
scene B: The run is over, but the name is bigger than it was. {N} has three meetings next week and means to make them count.
scene R: {N} wants the last night to last forever: the party, the dawn, the company one more time.
scene G: {N} feels the season turning. A run ends as a summer ends, and something else will grow.
scene tribal: At the end of the summer gathering the great mask is lifted from {Ns} face for the last time and hung back on its peg, and the band turns back to the hunt.
scene magic: On the last night the herald cries {Ns} name one more time, the patron's flowers wilt in the dressing room, and the wagons are already packed for the road.
- W | thank every member of the crew by name, from the stage | v: benevolence, conformity | tribal: thank every player and every keeper of the fire by name at the last fire | magic: thank every stagehand and player by name from the stage | chance: 92
- U | write down every lesson of the part before it fades | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | tribal: go over every lesson of the mask by the fire before it fades | magic: write every lesson of the part in your book before it fades | chance: 94
- B | line up the next three meetings before the set comes down | v: achievement, power | tribal: send a go-between to every band before the camp breaks, to ask for your telling | magic: arrange meetings with three masters of the play before the wagons roll | chance: 90
- R | party with the company until dawn, and say goodbye to the part properly | v: hedonism | tribal: dance with the players at the last fire until dawn | magic: feast with the company until dawn in the empty playhouse | chance: 90
- G | take the week away with your partner, and let the part go | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: go back to your own hearth with your partner, and let the mask go | magic: go to the sea with your partner for a week, and let the part go | chance: 80
- W>B | thank the director and producers in writing, and offer to work with them again | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: take a gift to the shaper and to the elder who gathered the hides, and offer to tell for them again | magic: write to the master of the play and the patron with thanks, and offer to play for them again | chance: 80
- U>R | travel somewhere new for a month, to see how others make theatre | door | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: walk alone to the far valleys for a moon, to see how other bands tell | magic: travel to a city you have never seen, to see how its players work | chance: 70
- B>G | save most of the run's money for the lean months that always follow | v: security | self_control: + | tribal: lay by the summer's gifts for the lean moons that always follow | magic: put the season's wages by for the lean months that always follow | chance: 80
- R>W | tell the truth in the last-night speech: what was hard, and who held the show up | v: benevolence, universalism | tribal: tell the band at the last fire what was hard, and who held the telling up | magic: tell the company in the last-night speech what was hard, and who held the show up | chance: 82
- G>U | go back to your old drama teacher, and talk the whole run through | v: tradition, achievement | tribal: go back to the old teller who taught you, and tell the whole summer through | magic: go back to the old master who taught you, and talk the whole season through | chance: 85
works: The last night ends well, and {N} walks out of the stage door into the morning with the part folded away like a good coat.
works: {partner} and {N} have the week away, and by the third day {N} sleeps through the night.
fails: The morning after, the empty diary feels like a fall, and {N} spends the week waiting for the phone.
fails: The last night goes on too long, and something said at the party follows {N} into the next job.

# ================================================================ director (ages 20-100)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 86. step 1 (cycle s4)

== the first day of rehearsals
tier: inner
threshold: title:director
step: 1
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-100
times: per new director: once, in the first weeks after taking the title; about 1 life in 1,000 (catalogue share; estimate)
holds: director
stakes: .8
tone: joy
life: work
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague
earth: the first day of rehearsals: a rehearsal room, a model box of the set, and a cast waiting to hear what you think the play is about
tribal: the first fire of the first telling you shape, with the whole band gathered and the players waiting for your first word
magic: the first day with the first company you direct, in a playhouse that has seen a hundred masters of the play
requires: in the threshold season of director, within the first six months after taking the title (opened by 'a director is needed for the autumn play' or any other way in): step 1, the first day of rehearsals of the first production
likelier: a first paid production; a cast of experienced actors; an actor, a stage manager or a teacher who has crossed over to directing
rarer: a director who has already run many amateur productions; a first film with no rehearsals at all
scene: The first day of rehearsals: a room above a pub, a model box of the set on a trestle table, coffee in paper cups and a cast of eight waiting to hear what {N} thinks the play is about. {N} has rewritten the opening talk four times. {boss}, the producer, sits at the back with a notebook.
scene W: {N} has drawn up the rehearsal schedule to the quarter hour, with the breaks where the actors' union says they should be.
scene U: {N} has read the play forty times and has a reading of it that fits on one page, and a second page of doubts.
scene B: This production is the calling card. The producer in the corner is deciding whether there will be a second one.
scene R: {N} cannot wait to get the actors on their feet. The talk can wait; the play is in their bodies, not on the page.
scene G: {N} thinks of the directors who taught by example, and of the old rehearsal rooms where {N} first sat at the side and watched.
scene tribal: At the first fire of the new telling the band gathers to see what {N} will make of the old story, and the players wait for {Ns} first word.
scene magic: In a playhouse that has seen a hundred masters of the play, the company waits on its benches for {Ns} first word, and the carved faces on the walls seem to wait too.
- W | set the schedule and the rules of the room, and keep them from the start | v: conformity, security | tribal: set out how the telling will be made and the customs of the fire, and keep them | magic: set the schedule and the rules of the company, and keep them from the first bell | chance: 90
- U | give the cast your one-page reading of the play, and ask them to argue with it | v: universalism, achievement | tribal: tell the players what you think the story means, and ask them to argue with it | chance: 84
- B | make sure the producer sees the cast laughing and working by lunchtime | v: power, achievement | tribal: make sure the elders see the players laughing and working by midday | magic: make sure the patron's man sees the company laughing and working by noon | chance: 82
- R | skip the talk, and get the actors on their feet in the first ten minutes | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: skip the speeches, and have the players moving in the firelight at once | chance: 92
- G | open with a circle where everyone says why they came to this play | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: open with the old circle, everyone saying which of their ancestors loved this story | magic: open with a circle where each player says why they came to this play | chance: 90
- W>G | take the whole company, crew included, to see the place where the play is set | door | v: benevolence, universalism | tribal: walk the whole band of players to the place where the story happened | magic: walk the whole company to the old quarter where the play is set | chance: 62
- U>W | go through the text line by line around the table for the whole first week | v: conformity, achievement | self_control: + | tribal: go through the telling word by word at the fire for the first moon | magic: go through the text line by line at the table for the whole first week | chance: 82
- B>U | spend half the spare budget on the best movement coach in the city | v: achievement, power | tribal: give half the spare hides to the best dancer of the valley, to teach the players | magic: spend half the spare purse on the best master of movement in the city | chance: 85
- R>B | announce on day one that this production will do what nobody has dared with the play | v: stimulation, power | mark: took a wild risk | tribal: declare at the first fire that this telling will do what no shaper has dared | magic: declare on the first day that this staging will do what no master has dared | chance: 55
- G>R | start each day with the old games your first company played, and let the room laugh | habit | v: tradition, hedonism | tribal: start each day with the old games the children of your band play, and let the players laugh | magic: start each day with the old games of your first pageant players, and let the room laugh | chance: 90
works: By the end of the first day the cast is on its feet, the play has started to breathe, and {boss} closes the notebook and smiles.
works: An actor stops {N} on the stairs to say it has been years since a first day felt like this.
fails: The opening talk runs long, the cast's eyes glaze over, and the day ends with nobody quite sure what play they are in.
fails: {N} says something on the first day that the cast repeats in the pub that night, and not kindly.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 87. step 2 (cycle s1)

== the designer who wants a different show
tier: inner
threshold: title:director
step: 2
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-100
times: per new director: once, in the first months of the threshold season, about ten weeks in; about 1 life in 1,000 (catalogue share; estimate)
holds: director
stakes: .8
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: months
roles: colleague, boss
earth: ten weeks in, the designer's final model shows a different show from the one you are making, and the workshop starts building on Monday
tribal: two moons in, the maker of the masks and screens has made them for a different telling from the one you are shaping
magic: ten weeks in, the playhouse's illusionist has woven a glamour for a different play from the one you are staging
requires: in the threshold season of director, within the first six months after taking the title: step 2, some weeks in, when the design and the staging pull apart
likelier: a designer older and better known than the director; a producer who loves the design; little money for changes
rarer: a director who designs their own shows; a bare-stage production with no set to speak of
scene: Ten weeks in, {colleague}, the designer, unveils the final model box, and it is beautiful, and it is a different show: a bare, empty room where {N} imagined a crowded kitchen. The workshop starts building on Monday. The designer is older, admired and sure, and {boss} loves the model and says it will photograph well.
scene W: There was an agreement: the first design meeting, the brief, the drawings everyone signed. {N} wants to know what happened to it.
scene U: {N} studies the model for an hour and has to admit it is interesting. The question is whether it serves the play.
scene B: Whoever wins this argument runs the production, and {N} knows the whole team is watching.
scene R: {N} hates it on sight, and has to sit on both hands not to say so in front of everyone.
scene G: {N} thinks of the play as written: a family, a kitchen, a table they have eaten at for thirty years. The empty room has no roots.
scene tribal: The maker of masks and screens has painted them for a different telling: no forest and no river, only a bare wall of hide where {N} wanted the hunt.
scene magic: The playhouse's illusionist has woven a glamour for a different play: a palace of light where {N} wanted a smoky inn.
- W | accept the producer's ruling on the design, though it is not your show | v: conformity | mark: gave in to pressure | self_control: - | tribal: accept the elders' word on the masks, though it is not your telling | magic: accept the play-merchant's ruling on the glamour, though it is not your play | chance: 88
- U | spend a day in the room testing both versions with the actors | door | v: achievement, universalism | tribal: try both tellings at the fire with the players for a day | magic: try both stagings with the company for a day, the glamour and the plain | chance: 75
- B | go to the producer first, and get the final say on design put in your hands | v: power | tribal: go first to the elder who gathered the hides, and get the last word put in your hands | magic: go first to the play-merchant, and get the last word put in your hands | chance: 62
- R | tell the designer straight out, in front of everyone, that it is the wrong show | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: made an enemy | tribal: tell the maker straight out at the fire that it is the wrong telling | magic: tell the illusionist before the whole company that it is the wrong play | chance: 40
- G | wait a week before anyone decides, and sleep on it | v: tradition, security | tribal: wait a moon before anyone decides, and let the fire settle it | chance: 55
- W>U | go through the play scene by scene against the drawings everyone signed | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: go through the telling part by part against what was agreed at the fire | magic: go through the play scene by scene against the drawings everyone sealed | chance: 70
- U>B | find the one change that makes the design serve your staging, and trade for it | v: achievement, power | tribal: find the one change that would serve your telling, and bargain the maker into it | magic: find the one change that would serve your staging, and bargain the illusionist into it | chance: 65
- B>R | wait for the technical rehearsal, and quietly cut whatever you dislike then | v: power, self-direction | tribal: wait for the last practice at the fire, and quietly leave out whatever you dislike then | magic: wait for the last rehearsal with the glamour, and quietly cut whatever you dislike then | chance: 75
- R>G | take the designer to the pub, and talk about your own family's kitchen | v: benevolence | mark: made a friend | tribal: sit with the maker by the river, and talk of your own family's fire | magic: take the illusionist to the tavern, and talk of your own family's kitchen | chance: 66
- G>W | ask the old head of the workshop how such quarrels were settled here before | door | v: tradition, conformity | tribal: ask the oldest keeper of the masks how such quarrels were settled before | magic: ask the playhouse's oldest stage-wright how such quarrels were settled before | chance: 75
works: Director and designer find a show they can both believe in, and the workshop builds it.
works: The cast walks into the finished set for the first time and goes quiet, in the good way.
fails: The design goes ahead as it was, and {N} spends the rest of rehearsals directing around it.
fails: The quarrel spreads to the whole team, and the production starts to split into sides.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 88. step 2, transform (cycle s2)

== the show you want to make
tier: inner
threshold: title:director
step: 2
transform: yes
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-100
times: per new director: once in the threshold season, about four months in, for most who are still open to change; about 1 life in 1,000 (catalogue share; estimate)
holds: director
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: work, meaning
horizon: years
roles: mentor, boss
earth: four months in, a producer asks over lunch what kind of work you want to make, and the answer will shape every room you run
tribal: four moons in, the elders ask what tellings you will shape now, and the next summer will follow your answer
magic: four months in, a patron asks over supper what plays you mean to make, and his purse waits on the answer
requires: in the threshold season of director, within the first six months after taking the title: step 2, the season's transforming chance, when the next production is offered and the director must say what work they make
likelier: a first production that went well; a producer or a theatre that asks what comes next; a director with actors around them already
rarer: a director with nothing offered; a director hired for one show only
scene: Four months in, {boss}, a producer, has asked {N} to lunch to talk about the next production, and over coffee the question comes: what kind of work does {N} want to make? {mentor}, the old director who gave {N} a first chance, once said that every director makes one show over and over all their life. {N} would like to choose which.
scene W: {N} thinks of the rehearsal room as a small society: fair hours, clear rules, everyone heard. A good room makes good work.
scene U: {N} thinks of the great plays nobody has truly understood yet, and wants to spend a life inside them.
scene B: {N} thinks of the directors whose names sell tickets, and of the bigger stages that come with a name.
scene R: {N} wants to make shows that frighten people, that set rooms alight, that nobody forgets.
scene G: {N} thinks of the actors from the first show, already like a family, and of a town that could have its own company for years.
scene tribal: Four moons in, the elders ask {N} at the fire what tellings {N} will shape now. The next summer will follow the answer.
scene magic: Four months in, a patron leans across the supper table and asks what plays {N} means to make, and his purse waits on the answer.
- W | run fair, ordered rooms where everyone is heard, whatever the play | identity | v: universalism, conformity | tribal: shape every telling by the fire's customs, where every player is heard | magic: run fair, ordered companies where every player is heard, whatever the play | chance: 80
- U | serve the great plays: read each one until it gives up its meaning, and stage that | identity | v: achievement, universalism | tribal: serve the oldest tellings: listen to each until it gives up its meaning, and shape that | magic: serve the great plays of the realm: read each until it gives up its meaning | chance: 72
- B | build a name: take every bigger stage offered, and make the shows people talk about | identity | v: achievement, power | tribal: build a name: take every greater fire offered, and shape the tellings every band talks about | magic: build a name: take every greater playhouse offered, and make the plays the court talks about | chance: 62
- R | make raw, dangerous shows, and never play it safe | identity | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: took a wild risk | tribal: shape wild, frightening tellings, and never tell it safe | chance: 72
- G | build a company of the same actors and designers, show after show | identity | binds | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: gather the same players around you, telling after telling | magic: gather a company of the same players and makers, play after play | chance: 65
- W>B | found a company with a written charter, fair pay and your name on the door | identity | binds | v: power, conformity | grants: a company of your own | tribal: gather players under customs you set, with fair shares and your name on the telling | magic: found a licensed company with a charter, fair wages and your name on the wagon | chance: 40
- U>R | commit to three years of new plays with young writers, and learn by failing | identity | binds | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: promise three summers of new tellings with the young makers, and learn by failing | magic: promise three seasons of new plays with young play-makers, and learn by failing | chance: 60
- B>G | sign on as the regular director of one small theatre, a show there every year | identity | binds | v: power, tradition | tribal: become one band's shaper of tellings for good, and make the tellings yours | magic: sign on as the regular master of the play at one small town playhouse | chance: 58
- R>W | direct only plays that say what is wrong in the world, and say it loud | identity | binds | v: universalism, stimulation | tribal: shape only tellings that say what is wrong in the band, and say it loud | magic: stage only plays that say what is wrong in the realm, and say it loud | chance: 78
- G>U | go back to the folk plays of your region, and learn to stage them anew | identity | v: tradition, achievement | tribal: go back to the oldest tellings of your own band, and learn to shape them anew | magic: go back to the old mystery plays of your region, and learn to stage them anew | chance: 72
works: The answer holds, and within the year the next production looks like the work {N} chose.
works: {mentor} sees the next show and sends {N} a short note: yes, that one.
fails: {N} says yes to everything offered, and the next year's work looks like nobody's in particular.
fails: The choice is made, and the first producer who hears it says politely that there is no money for that.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 89. step 3 (cycle s3)

== press night
tier: inner
threshold: title:director
step: 3
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-100
times: per new director: once, about five months in, near the end of the threshold season; about 1 life in 1,000 (catalogue share; estimate)
holds: director
stakes: .8
tone: mixed
life: work, public life, family
horizon: months
roles: partner, boss, colleague
earth: five months in, press night: critics in the third row, the producer pacing at the back, the cast in your hands, and your partner in the seat you saved
tribal: five moons in, the first great telling you have shaped, with the elders of every band at the fire to judge it
magic: five months in, opening night before the city: the patron in his box, and the broadsheet men in the pit
requires: in the threshold season of director, within the first six months after taking the title: step 3, near the end of the six months, on the press night of the first production
likelier: a professional production that critics review; a partner or family who come; a producer who must decide on the next show
rarer: an amateur or touring production no critic sees; a show that opened quietly
scene: Press night. The critics are in, the producer paces at the back of the stalls, and the cast are in their dressing rooms with {Ns} cards on their mirrors. A director's work is done before the curtain goes up, and that is the hard part: {N} can only watch. {partner} sits in the seat {N} saved, holding {Ns} hand too tightly.
scene W: {N} has done everything properly: the notes given, the cards written, the crew thanked. Now the show belongs to the actors.
scene U: {N} watches the critics as much as the stage, trying to read what they see.
scene B: Tomorrow's notices decide the next job, and {N} has already planned what to say to the press in the bar afterwards.
scene R: {N} cannot sit still, and watches the first act standing at the back, mouthing every line.
scene G: {N} thinks of the play as something that will live on without {N} now, like a child leaving home.
scene tribal: At the first great telling {N} has shaped, the elders of every band sit closest to the fire, and {N} can only watch from the dark.
scene magic: On opening night the patron leans out of his box, the broadsheet men fill the pit, and {N} can only watch from the wings.
- W | leave it to the actors, and sit quietly in your seat through the whole show | v: conformity, security | self_control: + | tribal: leave it to the players, and sit still at the edge of the firelight till the end | magic: leave it to the players, and sit quietly in your box through the whole play | chance: 75
- U | watch from the back and write notes for tomorrow, whatever the papers say | v: achievement | tribal: watch from the dark and remember every fault, to tell the players tomorrow | magic: watch from the back and write notes for tomorrow, whatever the broadsheets say | chance: 90
- B | take the credit for the designer's best idea in the interviews afterwards | v: power, achievement | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: claiming a colleague's idea as one's own; backfire: the designer reads the interview, and the theatre world hears of it | tribal: tell the elders afterwards that the maker's best idea was yours | magic: tell the broadsheet men that the illusionist's best idea was yours | chance: 90
- R | watch standing at the back, mouthing every line, and hug every actor at the interval | v: stimulation, benevolence | tribal: stand in the dark mouthing every word, and embrace every player when the telling breaks | magic: watch from the wings mouthing every line, and embrace every player at the interval | chance: 95
- G | sit with your partner and family, and let the night be theirs too | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: sit with your own kin at the fire, and let the night be theirs too | magic: sit with your household in the gallery, and let the night be theirs too | chance: 92
- W>R | give a speech at the party thanking every last person, and then dance | v: benevolence, hedonism | tribal: thank every player and helper at the feast, and then dance | chance: 92
- U>G | read the notices with the cast the next morning, and talk them through together | v: benevolence, achievement | tribal: hear the elders' judgment with the players at dawn, and talk it through together | magic: read the broadsheets with the company the next morning, and talk them through together | chance: 70
- B>W | introduce every actor to the producers and casting directors at the party | v: benevolence, power | tribal: bring every player before the elders of the other bands at the feast | magic: present every player to the patrons and brokers at the supper | chance: 80
- R>U | slip out at the interval, walk round the block, and come back with fresh eyes | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: slip away from the fire at the break, walk to the river and back, and watch it fresh | magic: slip out of the playhouse at the interval, walk round the square, and watch it fresh | chance: 90
- G>B | take the producer home to dinner with your family, and talk about the next show there | v: benevolence, achievement | tribal: bring the elder who gathered the hides to your own kin's fire, and talk of the next telling there | magic: bring the patron to supper with your household, and talk of the next play there | chance: 55
works: The notices are good, the cast is happy, and the producer rings at breakfast about a second show.
works: {partner} says on the way home that it was the best night out they have ever had, and {N} believes it.
fails: One notice dismisses the show in a paragraph, and the cast reads it before {N} does.
fails: The night goes well and the papers do not, and {N} learns how little a director controls after the curtain goes up.
